{"answer":"Flask integrates Jinja2 by creating its own Jinja2 environment subclass (flask.templating.Environment) that is initialized with the Flask app. If no loader is supplied, Flask uses the app's create_global_jinja_loader to build a DispatchingJinjaLoader which knows how to search the application's template folder and all blueprint template folders. The loader is attached to the environment as app.jinja_env.

When render_template (or render_template_string / stream_template) is called, Flask gets the current app via current_app, selects the requested template from app.jinja_env and then calls an internal _render helper. _render updates the template context with the standard Flask globals (request, session, g) via app.update_template_context, sends the before_render_template signal, renders the Jinja2 template, then sends the template_rendered signal before returning the rendered string (or iterator for streaming). This wiring gives Flask automatic access to Jinja2’s rendering engine while injecting Flask-specific context and lifecycle hooks.
"}

